From: WinBoat
Subject: [PATCH] d3d10umd: bob deinterlacing in the video processor

The video processor offered no deinterlacing: interlaced video through it
(Media Foundation's playback, players using the D3D11 video processor)
came out combed. It now offers bob (the rate conversion caps), and
VideoProcessorSetStreamFrameFormat takes the stream's field order.

Gallium's compositor bobs interlaced video buffers only; ours are frames
with the fields' rows interleaved. So for an interlaced stream a draw on
the video context first copies the field the output index names (0 the
first field, 1 the second) into a picture of half the height, every
other row of each plane (4:2:0 chroma rows alternate between the fields
too), and the compositor scales that one.
---
diff --git a/src/gallium/frontends/d3d10umd/Video.cpp b/src/gallium/frontends/d3d10umd/Video.cpp
index 2ba6b23..384fce0 100644
--- a/src/gallium/frontends/d3d10umd/Video.cpp
+++ b/src/gallium/frontends/d3d10umd/Video.cpp
@@ -31,6 +31,7 @@
